te lacks that trait. Another panel member may have relevant information.\" So we can treat \"100% reliable\" as a claim from Agent 1, but not a direct fact from original notes. However we can still reference it as part of the discussion. But we must be careful not to assert it as a fact we have. We can say \"Agent 1 noted that Candidate C is described as 100% reliable\". That's okay.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Likely we will align with C, but we could also raise concerns about communication and egocentric, maybe propose B? But B has nasty remarks, which is severe. D has arrogance and weak leadership. A is unknown. So C still seems best.\n\nThus we will vote Candidate C.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: mention Agent 1's and Agent 3's points, and maybe ask about missing info for A? But we need to keep it focused.\n\nWe can say: I agree with C, but we still need to explore A's reliability and stress resilience before final. But we must give a recommendation now.\n\nThus public message: I agree with C, because reliability, decision quality, calmness, technical competence, positive crew atmosphere outweigh communication issues, especially compared to B's disrespectful remarks and D's arrogance.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: B makes nasty remarks, D regarded as arrogant, C can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate C\"."}
